---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "Why AI Coding Agents Need a Health App — Harness Dreams";
const description =
  "A product essay on why AI coding agents need longitudinal health tracking instead of only per-session chat history.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="article-page">
      <header class="article-hero">
        <div class="container section-narrow">
          <span class="eyebrow">Blog / Product note</span>
          <h1>Why AI coding agents need a health app.</h1>
          <p class="lead">
            The useful question is not “was this one answer good?” It is “is
            this collaboration pattern getting healthier over time?”
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="container article-grid">
        <aside class="article-aside card">
          <img
            src="/story-mobile-review.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="aside-image"
          />
          <span class="mono">Thesis</span>
          <strong>Agent quality is longitudinal.</strong>
          <p>Repeated patterns matter more than isolated wins or misses.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="article-body">
          <p>
            AI coding tools already generate plenty of output. What they often
            lack is a tracker. A harness can ship a patch, run tools, and
            explain itself, but still leave you with no comparable sense of
            whether the day was smooth, wasteful, aligned, or turbulent.
          </p>

          <h2>Health is a trend, not a transcript</h2>
          <p>
            A single transcript is useful for debugging a moment. Health
            tracking is useful for changing a habit. Harness Dreams looks
            across sessions for metrics and repeated shapes: re-asks, hedges,
            failed tools, cache reuse, focus blocks, harness switches, and the
            agent thoughts hidden in transcript history.
          </p>

          <h2>Communication should be calm</h2>
          <p>
            The user should not wake up to a dense analytics dashboard. The
            Summary should feel closer to Health: one score, pinned cards,
            trends, highlights, quotes, and the occasional advice card when the
            evidence is strong.
          </p>

          <h2>The goal is better collaboration</h2>
          <p>
            The best recommendations are downstream of the tracker. The score
            explains what happened. The code-Code Flow graph shows when it happened.
            Quotes make it memorable. Advice stays small until the tracker has
            earned enough trust to ask for action.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</SiteLayout>
